  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nConditions are nice and quiet out there this morning as we're \noff to a mild start to our Sunday. Relatively cooler, but still \nseasonably warm, weather is in store for today following a \nstretch a hot, humid conditions. High temperatures are forecast \nin the lower 80s today away from the lake and middle to upper \n70s closer to the lakeshore. Dewpoints are still hanging out in \nthe upper 60s this morning, but steady dry advection from high \npressure building in from the northeast should pull dews down \ninto the upper 50s by late this afternoon. This means we're not \nlooking at much of a heat index factor today and conditions \nshould feel close to what the thermometer reads.\n\nBreezy ENE winds over the lake have gradually been subsiding \nthrough the night and waves have responded similarly. Moderate \nwave action will likely be present at Illinois beaches through \nthe morning which could make swimming hazardous for those who \naren't strong swimmers. So we're advertising a Moderate Swim \nRisk to start the day at Illinois beaches, although conditions \nwill be easing through the day. Conditions look generally \nfavorable for Labor Day tomorrow, but keep in mind high \ntemperatures are forecast in the 70s both today and tomorrow \nalong the lakeshore, maybe close to 80 tomorrow at Indiana \nbeaches.\n\nSimilar to today, temperatures for Labor Day are forecast in \nthe lower 80s away from the lake. High pressure over the \nnorthern Great Lakes will keep rain chances away and bring \nmostly sunny skies.\n\nDuring the middle of the week, a tighly-wound upper low will \ntrack east across Ontario and a cold front will get dragged \nacross the area sometime Wednesday bringing our next opportunity\nfor rain and thunderstorms. Temperatures should trend up again \nbeginning on Tuesday in anticipation of this system with highs \nforecast in the middle and upper 80s on Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThere remains a great deal of uncertainty regarding this system \nfrom the timing of the frontal passage to the ambient \nenvironment near the front. Lapse rates aloft look generally \npoor as the cooler mid level air looks to stay wrapped up in the\nupper low to the north, and a prominent high across the SE US \nmay also impinge on low level moisture feed ahead of the front \nfurther constraining instability. There will also be a diurnal \nfactor to consider but is not yet clear. Considerable spread \nexists even within individual ensemble system regarding the \nthermodynamic environment. The Euro and GFS also have quite \ndifferent ideas on the kinematic environment around the front, \nso it's unclear what quality of shear or kinematic forcing we'll\nhave to work with, but the more supportive shear should also \nfocus to our north near the base of the upper low. The more \nbullish scenarios do support a marginal severe threat for \nWednesday, so something to keep an eye on over the next few \ndays.\n\nThe main upper trough axis will swing over early Thursday and \ndrive a secondary cold front across the area, but layer moisture\nlooks to get shunted to our south after Wednesday and precip \npotential currently looks low during the day on Thursday. Behind\nthe departing trough, a series of shortwave impulses traversing\na generally zonal upper flow pattern will drive sporadic rain \nchances through next weekend with a larger synoptic system \nfavored by medium range guidance to traverse the region in the \nlate Sunday-Monday window.\n\nDoom",
